@@s001 Code: Ford-Fulkerson in Java
@@s002 Code: Ford-Fulkerson in Java
@@s003 The maximum flow with augmenting paths: matrix of residual capacities, BFS on the residual network, bottleneck and cancellation of the flow.
@@s004 The residual network
@@s005 The residual capacity is a matrix: <code>cap[u][v]</code> is how much I can still send from u to v. Suitable for small graphs.
@@s006 The residual network
@@s007 n×n matrix: <code>cap[u][v]</code> is the capacity of the edge u→v, 0 if there is none.
@@s008 <code>+=</code> for parallel edges: two edges u→v add up.
@@s009 Syntax notes
@@s010 <code>static</code> on the class's global variables: the <code>main</code> method uses them without passing them.
@@s011 Augmenting path
@@s012 A BFS on the residual network finds a path s→t. The bottleneck is the minimum capacity along the path: that amount passes.
@@s013 Augmenting path
@@s014 The bottleneck starts from the maximum: each edge of the path reduces it.
@@s015 The forward edge loses capacity.
@@s016 The backward edge gains: it is the cancellation of the flow, the residual network.
@@s017 Syntax notes
@@s018 The <code>for</code> that climbs the parents goes from <code>t</code> to <code>s</code>: to reconstruct the path backwards.
@@s019 Running it
@@s020 Example of input and output (actually compiled and run).
